 Each game needs a different strategy, which is what makes them good discussion starters:
 
 - **Ice** rewards slow cooling from warm (annealing), then staying cold: going silent at once freezes a disordered jumble, and holding one temperature grows too little. A short reheating can melt defects so they regrow in order.
-- **Separate the liquids** rewards a temperature that is cold enough to separate but warm enough for molecules to move: too cold and everything freezes in place.
+- **Separate oil from water** rewards a temperature that is cold enough to separate but warm enough for molecules to move: too cold and everything freezes in place.
 - **Deliver the drug** rewards timing: the micelles need a moment to open, so start the noise just before each window and stop just before it ends. Its challenges switch to a tumour zone (see Challenges).
 - **Gel strand** rewards heat-then-cool: folded proteins cannot link, and too much heat breaks the links.
 
+### Daily Molecule
+
+After the first finished game, a **📅 Daily Molecule** button sits above the list of games. Every day one of the four games is chosen (they take turns), and everyone who plays that day starts from exactly the same arrangement of molecules (the start comes from the date; in the drug game, the target windows too). Students can play it as often as they like: the best score of the day is kept and shown on the button. Playing on days in a row builds a streak (🔥 3 days in a row). Stars, cards and statistics count as in a normal game. Nothing is sent anywhere: two students compare by sharing their result.
+
+### Sharing a result and challenge links
+
+The end card of a normal game, a speed run and the Daily Molecule has **📤 Share**. A shared speed run becomes a *speed challenge*: the friend plays the same speed run from the same start, and must reach the goal faster. It opens a window with:
+- **A short text** with the game, the score and the stars, and a **challenge link**. *Share…* uses the device's own share menu (phones, tablets), *Copy text and link* puts it on the clipboard.
+- **The clip** (when *Record a clip to share* is ticked): a 30-second vertical video of the run (box, temperature bar, score, stars), ready for a message or a short-video app. *Save the clip* downloads it (MP4 where the browser can make it, otherwise WebM).
+
+Whoever opens the challenge link sees *🏆 You have been challenged!* with the score to beat. *Accept the challenge* starts the same game from exactly the same molecules, and the friend's run is shown as **😊** on the score bar at the same second (like the 👻 of your own best run; the link carries the friend's score for each second, 31 numbers). The end card then says whether they beat it, and they can share their own score back. A new student who opens a challenge for the ice game first goes through the guided microphone set-up, as on any first visit. The link opens the public page (https://dmtolmach.github.io/be-quite-and-self-assemble/) when the game was played from a file or inside claude.ai, and the school's own page when it is served from the school's website.
+
+Same start does not mean identical runs: the starting arrangement and the drug windows are the same for everyone, but each player's temperatures differ (and a slow computer may run fewer steps per picture), so the molecules soon go their own way. That is the point: the better temperature control wins.
+
 ### Challenges
 
 When all six cards of a game's deck are collected, that game opens five **challenges** (listed under the game picker). Each level is harder than the one before; a star clears a level and opens the next, and cleared levels can be replayed at any time. Challenges show no tips, coach line or diagnosis, and do not change the statistics.
